ozz-archive-invariants: refuse what Load reads that the jobs cannot walk

Upstream Load trusts the content of an archive once its counts fit. A
skeleton's last name may run past its name block, which the pointer fixup
reads with strlen; a joint's parent may be a later joint or out of range,
which LocalToModelJob indexes its output by; a track's first ratio may be
past 0, from which TrackSamplingJob steps back one key before the first;
an animation's keys may name no timepoint, break the chains of previous
keys sampling walks, or carry iframes holding other keys, descriptors not
in pairs, or an interval seeking past the last iframe, each of which
SamplingJob reads past a buffer by.

Skeleton::Load refuses a name with no terminator inside the block and a
parent that is neither kNoParent nor an earlier joint. Track::Load refuses
ratios that do not start at 0 and ascend within [0, 1]. Animation::Load
refuses timepoints that do not ascend within [0, 1], and per channel: fewer
than two keys per track, a ratio index past the timepoints, a first key not
at time 0, a second key not right after its track's first, a later key not
stepping back to its own track's latest key, descriptors not in pairs, an
interval that seeks past the last iframe, and an iframe whose entries do not
decode within the entry bytes into the cache that walk reaches at its key.
The walk's scratch comes from ozz's allocator and a refused one is
IArchive::FailAllocation. A refused Load leaves its object empty:
Deallocate now clears every span over the block it frees, so an object
that held content reports none.

Evidence: zozz's src/integration_test.zig loads one archive per refusal,
counts honest and content broken, with each of ozz's blocks ending at an
inaccessible page (shakedown's Quarantine), then runs the job upstream would:
upstream faults at the guard page or asserts an index out of range in every
case that reads past a buffer, and loads the rest clean (measured,
2026-10-31); tests/reload.cpp shows a refused reload leaves an object empty.

